{"reference":"Zechariah 11","book":"Zechariah","chapter":11,"status":"pending","sections":[{"kind":"interpretive_questions","title":"The Shepherding Signs","blocks":[{"id":"block_1","text":"The chapter presents its message through a sequence of shepherding signs. Zechariah is told to tend a flock marked for slaughter or exploitation, using two staffs called Favor and Union; both are later broken. The shepherd’s wages—thirty pieces of silver—are then thrown to the potter or treasury in the house of Jehovah. Finally, a foolish shepherd is portrayed as one who neglects, fails to seek, heal, or feed the vulnerable flock, and receives a woe. The precise interpretation of these signs is disputed, but their repeated contrast between care and exploitation is central to the chapter’s portrayal.","verse_refs":["Zechariah 11:12-13","Zechariah 11:15-17","Zechariah 11:4-14"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_zechariah_011_b63ad436b312cda48d9a1707"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]}],"generated_metadata":null}